                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 8.</seg></opener>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <head>Mr. Waddington to Mr. Hammond.</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Whitehall,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1864-02-19"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >February</hi> 19, 1864.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Referring to the previous correspondence relative to an alleged breach of the
                    foreign enlistment act by Mr. Patrick H. Finney, I am directed by Secretary Sir
                    George Grey to acquaint you, for the information of Earl Russell, that the
                    papers in the case having been laid before the law officers of the crown in
                    Ireland, they delivered an opinion, dated the 2d instant. The crown solicitor
                    having been instructed to act upon that opinion with regard to procuring further
                    evidence from the witness, William Pike, obtained from him a statement in
                    writing, which has also been submitted to the law officers, who are of opinion
                    that the evidence would not be sufficient to sustain a prosecution. I am,
                    &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>H. WADDINGTON.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
